window.BioChecks[28] = {"2007-Q13a":[{"q":"Which blood vessel brings blood back to the heart from the lungs?","right":"Pulmonary vein","wrong":["Pulmonary artery","Vena cava","Aorta"],"why":"The pulmonary vein carries oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the heart; the pulmonary artery goes the other way."},{"q":"Which gas does the pulmonary vein mainly carry, and how?","right":"Oxygen, joined to haemoglobin in red cells","wrong":["Carbon dioxide, as hydrogencarbonate","Oxygen, carried by white blood cells","Carbon dioxide, joined to haemoglobin"],"why":"Blood leaving the lungs is full of oxygen, which binds to haemoglobin in red blood cells as oxyhaemoglobin."}],"2007-Q13b":[{"q":"On your diagram, which tube branches off the trachea into each lung?","right":"Bronchus","wrong":["Bronchiole","Oesophagus","Larynx"],"why":"The trachea splits into two bronchi, one for each lung; bronchioles are the smaller branches inside the lung."},{"q":"What is the job of the larynx?","right":"Makes sounds: it's the voice box","wrong":["Pushes food down to the stomach","Passes oxygen into the blood","Pumps air into the lungs"],"why":"The larynx is the voice box: air passing over its vocal cords makes them vibrate and make sound."},{"q":"In inhalation, what happens to the chest's volume and the air pressure inside?","right":"Volume increases, pressure decreases","wrong":["Volume decreases, pressure increases","Volume increases, pressure increases","Volume decreases, pressure decreases"],"why":"The muscles make the chest bigger, so the pressure inside drops below the air outside and air flows in."}],"2007-Q13c":[{"q":"Which is one way an alveolus is adapted for gas exchange?","right":"Its wall is only one cell thick","wrong":["It has a thick, muscular wall","It has a dry, waxy lining","It has valves to stop backflow"],"why":"A wall one cell thick means gases only travel a tiny distance to diffuse between the air and the blood."},{"q":"By what process do gases pass between the alveolus and the blood?","right":"Diffusion","wrong":["Osmosis","Active transport","Respiration"],"why":"Gases move from high to low concentration with no energy needed: that's diffusion. Osmosis is only for water."},{"q":"Which of these is a disorder of the breathing system?","right":"Asthma","wrong":["Anaemia","Angina","Arthritis"],"why":"Asthma affects the airways, so it's a breathing disorder; anaemia is a blood disorder and angina a heart one."},{"q":"In asthma, what causes the difficulty in breathing?","right":"Bronchioles narrow, so less air gets through","wrong":["Alveolar walls break down and merge","Cartilage rings in the trachea collapse","The diaphragm stops contracting"],"why":"Triggers like dust or pet dander make the bronchioles tighten and narrow, so air struggles to get through."}],"2009-Q13c":[{"q":"Which part is labelled A on the diagram?","right":"Bronchiole","wrong":["Bronchus","Alveolus","Capillary"],"why":"A is the narrow airway leading into the air sacs: a bronchiole. A bronchus is a much wider tube."},{"q":"Which feature shown on the diagram helps gas exchange?","right":"Capillaries are pressed close to alveoli","wrong":["Alveoli have thick, muscular walls","Alveoli have a dry inner lining","Capillaries have valves to slow blood"],"why":"With capillaries right against the alveoli, gases have only a tiny distance to diffuse."},{"q":"For asthma, which is a good way to prevent attacks?","right":"Avoid allergens such as dust and pet hair","wrong":["Take antibiotics every few weeks","Eat plenty of iron-rich food","Breathe through the mouth, not nose"],"why":"Asthma attacks are often set off by allergens, so staying away from them helps prevent attacks."},{"q":"A rise in which dissolved gas makes you breathe deeper or faster?","right":"Carbon dioxide","wrong":["Oxygen","Nitrogen","Water vapour"],"why":"A rise in CO2 in the blood is sensed by the brain, which speeds up breathing to get rid of it."}],"2010-Q7a":[{"q":"Which body cavity holds the heart and lungs?","right":"Thoracic (chest) cavity","wrong":["Abdominal cavity","Pelvic cavity","Cranial cavity"],"why":"The heart and lungs sit in the thorax (chest), above the diaphragm; the abdomen is below it."}],"2011-Q8b":[{"q":"Why do heart rate and breathing rate rise during exercise?","right":"Muscles need more oxygen for respiration","wrong":["Muscles need more CO2 for respiration","Lungs must absorb more glucose","Muscles make oxygen that must be removed"],"why":"Working muscles respire faster to release energy, so they need more oxygen and make more CO2 to remove."}],"2012-Q15c":[{"q":"How do the lungs help with homeostasis?","right":"They excrete carbon dioxide","wrong":["They excrete urea","They store glycogen","They produce insulin"],"why":"The lungs get rid of CO2 (and some water and heat), keeping blood CO2 steady. Urea goes out via the kidneys."}],"2014-Q9b":[{"q":"In the exercise investigation, what did you measure first?","right":"The resting pulse or breathing rate","wrong":["The rate during hard exercise","The recovery time after exercise","The person's body temperature"],"why":"You first find the rate at rest, so you have a baseline to compare with the rates after exercise."},{"q":"How do you measure pulse rate?","right":"Feel the pulse and count beats in a set time","wrong":["Feel the pulse and judge how strong it is","Count beats until the pulse slows down","Time how long you can hold your breath"],"why":"Find the pulse (e.g. at the wrist) and count the beats for a set time, like 60 s, to get beats per minute."}],"2014-Q12b":[{"q":"On your diagram, in what order does air pass through the breathing tract?","right":"Trachea, bronchus, bronchiole, alveolus","wrong":["Trachea, bronchiole, bronchus, alveolus","Bronchus, trachea, bronchiole, alveolus","Trachea, alveolus, bronchus, bronchiole"],"why":"The trachea splits into two bronchi, which branch into smaller bronchioles that end in alveoli."},{"q":"What starts the process of inhalation?","right":"The brain sends impulses to breathing muscles","wrong":["The lungs expand on their own","The heart sends impulses to the diaphragm","Air rushes in and pushes the ribs out"],"why":"The brain sends nerve impulses to the diaphragm and intercostals; the lungs have no muscle to expand themselves."}],"2014-Q12c":[{"q":"In which part of a cell is carbon dioxide made?","right":"Mitochondrion","wrong":["Ribosome","Chloroplast","Nucleus"],"why":"CO2 is released in stage 2 of aerobic respiration (the Krebs cycle), which happens in the mitochondrion."},{"q":"Which feature of a capillary helps it take up CO2 quickly?","right":"Its wall is only one cell thick","wrong":["Its wall is thick and muscular","It has valves along its length","It has a very wide lumen"],"why":"A wall one cell thick gives CO2 a very short distance to diffuse. Valves and wide lumens belong to veins."},{"q":"Why does venous blood usually carry more CO2 than arterial blood?","right":"It has collected CO2 from respiring cells","wrong":["It picked up CO2 from air in the lungs","Its oxygen has turned into CO2","Veins have valves that trap the CO2"],"why":"Body cells release CO2 from respiration into the blood; arterial blood was cleared of CO2 in the lungs."},{"q":"Which blood vessel is an exception to this rule?","right":"Pulmonary vein","wrong":["Vena cava","Aorta","Renal vein"],"why":"It's a vein, but its blood was just cleared of CO2 in the lungs. (The pulmonary artery also counts.)"},{"q":"What role does CO2 play in controlling breathing rate?","right":"Rising CO2 is detected and breathing speeds up","wrong":["Rising CO2 is detected and breathing slows down","Falling CO2 is detected and breathing speeds up","CO2 has no effect; only oxygen matters"],"why":"The medulla oblongata senses CO2 in the blood; more CO2 means faster breathing to get rid of it."}],"2016-Q8b":[{"q":"What was the control in this exercise investigation?","right":"The rate measured at rest","wrong":["The rate straight after exercise","The highest rate during exercise","The time taken to recover"],"why":"The resting rate is the control: the normal rate with no exercise, to compare the other results with."},{"q":"Why do you need this control?","right":"To compare with the rates after exercise","wrong":["To warm the person up before exercise","To find the person's highest rate","To keep the rate the same throughout"],"why":"Without the resting rate, you couldn't tell how much exercise changed the pulse or breathing rate."},{"q":"On the graph, how should B's curve compare with A's?","right":"B's rate rises more than A's","wrong":["A's rate rises more than B's","Both rise by exactly the same amount","B's rate falls during exercise"],"why":"Unfit B's heart and lungs must work harder for the same exercise, so B's rate climbs more than athlete A's."},{"q":"How could you measure recovery time?","right":"Time how long until the rate is back to resting","wrong":["Count the rate once, right after exercise","Time how long the exercise lasts","Time how long until the rate peaks"],"why":"Recovery time is how long the rate takes to get back to the resting rate, timed from the end of exercise."}],"2017-Q15a":[{"q":"During inhalation, what does the diaphragm do?","right":"Contracts and flattens, moving down","wrong":["Relaxes and flattens, moving down","Contracts and domes, moving up","Relaxes and domes, moving up"],"why":"The diaphragm contracts, pulling its dome flat and down, which makes the chest cavity bigger."},{"q":"Which of these is a human breathing disorder?","right":"Bronchitis","wrong":["Hepatitis","Gastritis","Appendicitis"],"why":"Bronchitis is inflamed bronchi, the airways into the lungs; the others affect the liver, stomach and gut."},{"q":"For bronchitis, what is a cause?","right":"Infection, or irritants like smoke","wrong":["A lack of iron in the diet","A blocked coronary artery","Eating too much salt in food"],"why":"Bronchitis is inflammation of the bronchi, caused by viruses or bacteria, or by irritants such as smoke."}],"2019-Q6":[{"q":"Which blood vessel does the lung capillary branch off from?","right":"Pulmonary artery","wrong":["Pulmonary vein","Aorta","Vena cava"],"why":"Blood reaches the lungs in the pulmonary artery, which branches into arterioles and then these capillaries."},{"q":"Which feature of the lung capillaries helps gas exchange?","right":"A dense network around each alveolus","wrong":["Thick, elastic, muscular walls","Valves to stop blood flowing back","A lining of beating cilia"],"why":"A dense net of capillaries wraps each alveolus, giving lots of surface for gases to cross."},{"q":"What is a common treatment for asthma?","right":"An inhaler (bronchodilator)","wrong":["A course of antibiotics","Insulin injections","Iron tablets"],"why":"An inhaler delivers a drug that relaxes and widens the bronchioles; antibiotics only kill bacteria."}],"2021-Q16d":[{"q":"Which structures at the end of the airways carry out gas exchange?","right":"Alveoli","wrong":["Bronchioles","Bronchi","Villi"],"why":"The airways end in millions of tiny air sacs called alveoli. Villi are the finger-like folds in the gut."},{"q":"Which feature of alveoli makes gas exchange efficient?","right":"A very large total surface area","wrong":["Lined with cilia to sweep out mucus","Rings of cartilage to keep them open","Thick walls that resist damage"],"why":"Millions of alveoli add up to a huge surface, so lots of gas can diffuse across at once."},{"q":"Which gas dissolved in plasma controls the rate of breathing?","right":"Carbon dioxide","wrong":["Oxygen","Nitrogen","Carbon monoxide"],"why":"The brain mainly monitors CO2 in the plasma; more CO2 means you breathe faster to get rid of it."},{"q":"Which part of the brain detects this gas?","right":"Medulla oblongata","wrong":["Hypothalamus","Cerebellum","Cerebrum"],"why":"The medulla oblongata controls breathing and heart rate. The hypothalamus controls things like temperature."},{"q":"How does the breathing system respond to a high CO2 level?","right":"Breathing gets faster and deeper","wrong":["Breathing gets slower and shallower","Breathing gets slower but deeper","Breathing stops until CO2 falls"],"why":"Faster, deeper breaths get rid of the extra CO2 from the blood more quickly."},{"q":"Why does air flow into the lungs during inhalation?","right":"Chest pressure falls below the air outside","wrong":["Chest pressure rises above the air outside","The lungs' own muscles suck air in","The alveoli pump air in by squeezing"],"why":"The bigger chest lowers the pressure inside, so air from outside, at higher pressure, rushes in."}],"2023-Q17c":[{"q":"On your sketch, which way should the O2 and CO2 arrows point?","right":"O2 into the blood, CO2 into the alveolus","wrong":["CO2 into the blood, O2 into the alveolus","Both gases into the blood","Both gases into the alveolus"],"why":"O2 is high in the alveolus so it diffuses into the blood; CO2 is high in the blood so it diffuses out."},{"q":"Which diagram shows inhalation, and how can you tell?","right":"X, because the ribs move up and out","wrong":["Y, because the ribs move down and in","X, because the diaphragm moves up","Y, because the diaphragm moves down"],"why":"In X the ribs move up and out and the diaphragm moves down, so the chest gets bigger: that's inhalation."}],"2025-Q16b":[{"q":"What type of blood cell is C, the one that carries a gas?","right":"Red blood cell","wrong":["White blood cell","Platelet","Lymphocyte"],"why":"Red blood cells are packed with haemoglobin, which carries oxygen; white cells like lymphocytes fight infection."},{"q":"Which feature of structure B (the alveolus) helps gas exchange?","right":"A moist inner surface","wrong":["A thick layer of muscle","A lining of beating cilia","A wall stiffened by cartilage"],"why":"Gases dissolve in the moist lining first, which lets them diffuse across the thin wall."},{"q":"Which gas is X, judging by its direction on the diagram?","right":"Carbon dioxide, leaving the blood","wrong":["Oxygen, leaving the blood","Oxygen, entering the blood","Carbon dioxide, entering the blood"],"why":"Blood reaching the lungs is high in CO2, so CO2 diffuses out into the alveolus. O2 (Y) goes the other way."},{"q":"What is the movement of these gases called?","right":"Diffusion","wrong":["Osmosis","Active transport","Ventilation"],"why":"Each gas moves from high to low concentration across thin walls, with no energy used: that's diffusion."},{"q":"Which two muscles are directly involved in inhalation?","right":"Diaphragm and intercostal muscles","wrong":["Diaphragm and abdominal muscles","Intercostals and cardiac muscle","Diaphragm and trachea muscles"],"why":"The diaphragm and the intercostals (between the ribs) both contract to make the chest bigger."},{"q":"During exhalation, what do the breathing muscles do?","right":"Relax, so ribs fall and diaphragm rises","wrong":["Relax, so ribs rise and diaphragm falls","Contract, so ribs rise and diaphragm falls","Relax, so ribs fall and diaphragm flattens"],"why":"As the muscles relax, the ribs drop and the diaphragm domes up, so chest volume falls and air is pushed out."},{"q":"Which disorder is correctly matched with a treatment?","right":"Bronchitis: antibiotics, if bacterial","wrong":["Asthma: a course of antibiotics","Bronchitis: insulin injections","Asthma: iron tablets"],"why":"Antibiotics kill bacteria, so they treat bacterial bronchitis; asthma is treated with an inhaler instead."}],"2026-Q3":[{"q":"In which body cavity is the breathing system found?","right":"Thoracic (chest) cavity","wrong":["Abdominal cavity","Pelvic cavity","Cranial cavity"],"why":"The lungs and most of the airways sit in the thoracic cavity, protected by the ribs, above the diaphragm."},{"q":"What does the epiglottis do?","right":"Stops food going down the trachea","wrong":["Makes sounds in the voice box","Warms and filters incoming air","Pushes food down the oesophagus"],"why":"When you swallow, the epiglottis covers the top of the trachea, so food goes down the oesophagus instead."},{"q":"What do the rings of cartilage in the trachea do?","right":"Keep the trachea open so air can pass","wrong":["Close the trachea when you swallow","Sweep mucus up towards the throat","Squeeze to push air into the lungs"],"why":"The stiff C-shaped rings stop the trachea collapsing when the pressure inside drops as you breathe in."},{"q":"Where should you draw the diaphragm on the diagram?","right":"Just under the lungs, as a dome","wrong":["Just above the lungs, near the trachea","Between the two lungs","Lower down, below the stomach"],"why":"The diaphragm is a dome-shaped sheet of muscle right under the lungs, separating the chest from the abdomen."},{"q":"Which way should your arrow point for the diaphragm in inhalation?","right":"Downwards, away from the lungs","wrong":["Upwards, into the chest","Outwards, towards the ribs","Nowhere: only the ribs move"],"why":"When the diaphragm contracts it flattens and moves down, making the chest bigger."},{"q":"Apart from the diaphragm, which muscle helps you breathe in?","right":"Intercostal muscles","wrong":["Abdominal muscles","Cardiac muscle","Muscle in the trachea wall"],"why":"The intercostal muscles between the ribs contract to lift the rib cage up and out."},{"q":"What happens to the rib cage during inhalation?","right":"It moves up and out","wrong":["It moves down and in","It moves up and in","It moves down and out"],"why":"The intercostal muscles contract and lift the ribs up and out, which makes the chest bigger."}],"S1-Q3a":[{"q":"Which way should your O2 and CO2 arrows point on the diagram?","right":"O2 into the capillary, CO2 into the air space","wrong":["O2 into the air space, CO2 into the capillary","Both gases into the capillary","Both gases into the air space"],"why":"Each gas diffuses down its gradient: O2 is high in the alveolar air, CO2 is high in the blood."},{"q":"By what process does this gas exchange happen?","right":"Diffusion","wrong":["Osmosis","Active transport","Phagocytosis"],"why":"O2 and CO2 move from high to low concentration through the thin walls, using no energy: diffusion."},{"q":"How does a thin alveolar wall help gas exchange?","right":"Gases have only a short distance to diffuse","wrong":["Gases can be pumped across using energy","It stops water vapour from escaping","It keeps the inside surface dry"],"why":"The wall is one cell thick, so O2 and CO2 only travel a tiny distance, which makes diffusion fast."}],"S1-Q3c":[{"q":"What is the main way carbon dioxide is carried in the blood?","right":"As hydrogencarbonate ions in the plasma","wrong":["Mainly bound to haemoglobin","Carried inside white blood cells","As tiny gas bubbles in the plasma"],"why":"Most CO2 reacts with water to form hydrogencarbonate ions in the plasma; only some joins haemoglobin."},{"q":"What is the main way oxygen is carried in the blood?","right":"Bound to haemoglobin in red blood cells","wrong":["Mostly dissolved in the plasma","As hydrogencarbonate ions in plasma","Carried inside white blood cells"],"why":"Oxygen joins haemoglobin in red blood cells to form oxyhaemoglobin; very little dissolves in plasma."},{"q":"In the pulmonary artery (heart to lungs), what are the CO2 and O2 levels?","right":"CO2 high, oxygen low","wrong":["CO2 low, oxygen high","CO2 high, oxygen high","CO2 low, oxygen low"],"why":"This blood comes back from the body, where cells used up O2 and added CO2; it hasn't reached the lungs yet."},{"q":"Which axes should your graph of the table have?","right":"x: time (min); y: blood pH","wrong":["x: blood pH; y: time (min)","x: person (A or B); y: blood pH","x: time (min); y: person (A or B)"],"why":"Time is what you chose to vary, so it goes on x; blood pH is what you measured, so it goes on y."},{"q":"Who is better at removing CO2 from the blood, and why?","right":"A, as their pH stays in the normal range","wrong":["B, as their pH drops much further","A, as their pH rises during exercise","B, as their blood gets more acidic"],"why":"CO2 makes blood acidic. A's pH only falls to 7.35 (normal) but B's falls to 7.17, so CO2 builds up in B."}],"S1-Q3d":[{"q":"Which muscles cause inhalation, and are they contracting or relaxing?","right":"Diaphragm and intercostals, both contracting","wrong":["Diaphragm and intercostals, both relaxing","Diaphragm contracting, intercostals relaxing","Diaphragm relaxing, intercostals contracting"],"why":"Both muscles contract to breathe in: the diaphragm flattens and the intercostals lift the ribs."},{"q":"From the graph, roughly how much air goes in and out per breath at rest?","right":"About 0.5 L","wrong":["About 2.0 L","About 2.5 L","About 3.5 L"],"why":"At rest the trace goes from 2.0 L up to 2.5 L each breath, so about 0.5 L moves in and out."},{"q":"Where on the graph should you write X, the start of exercise?","right":"Where breaths start getting faster and deeper","wrong":["Where breaths return to slow and shallow","At the tallest peak of the trace","At the very start of the graph"],"why":"Exercise starts where the trace changes from slow, small waves to quick, big ones (about 15 s)."},{"q":"From the graph, how did the athlete's breathing change during exercise?","right":"Breaths got faster and deeper","wrong":["Breaths got faster but shallower","Breaths got slower but deeper","Breaths got slower and shallower"],"why":"The waves come closer together (higher rate) and get taller (more air per breath) during exercise."}],"S2-Q5a":[{"q":"How does the CO2 level in the plasma change from vessel A to vessel B?","right":"It rises: body cells add CO2 on the way","wrong":["It falls: the lungs remove CO2 on the way","It falls: body cells use up the CO2","It stays the same: CO2 stays in red cells"],"why":"A (aorta) carries blood fresh from the lungs, low in CO2; by B (pulmonary artery) it has CO2 from body cells."}]};
